	abstract = {Observability data (logs, metrics, and traces), as a direct reflection of the internal operational state of microservice systems, provide a critical foundation for real-time anomaly detection. This paper systematically reviews research progress in real-time anomaly detection driven by observability data in microservice systems. It first establishes a formal model of the constituent elements of microservice systems and provides unified definitions for the three types of observability data, laying a theoretical foundation for subsequent studies. On this basis, a formal definition of the real-time anomaly detection problem and a general solution framework are proposed. Guided by this framework, anomaly detection methods based on single data sources (logs, metrics, and traces) and multi-modal fusion are analyzed from multiple dimensions, with an in-depth exploration of the core concepts, technological evolution, and strengths and weaknesses of each approach. Finally, the key challenges in the field are summarized, and future research directions are outlined, providing theoretical references and practical guidance for building efficient and adaptive anomaly detection systems in microservices. © (2026), (Computer Engineering and Applications). All rights reserved.},
