	abstract = {Digital twins (DTs) enable the orchestration of heterogeneous data, models and services for decision-making and process optimization. However, creating a single DT for complex environments can be costly or impractical, motivating the use of composite DTs (CDTs) formed by integrating simpler DTs. This paper approaches the composition of DTs from a conceptual perspective. It analyzes core DT elements, introduces a formal composition framework defining relationships between DTs and classifying them as vertical, horizontal, or transversal. It also addresses key challenges, including scalability, interoperability, relationship modeling, and temporal management. To address these challenges, a scalable modeling approach is proposed based on a decentralized Web of Things graph that acts as a proxy of the real environment and is complemented by knowledge graphs (KGs) representing the semantic view of each DT. In addition, an event-driven and actor-oriented microservices architecture is presented, materializing in version 2 of the open-source platform OpenTwins. Experimental validation includes three studies: a comparative scalability analysis showing lower latency and higher reliability under heavy load, a qualitative evaluation confirming improved semantic representation using KGs, and a quantitative test demonstrating runtime adaptability. Overall, the work delivers a formalization, modeling approach, and scalable architecture for CDT development in complex domains. © 1976-2012 IEEE.},
