	pages = {1043 - 1048},
	doi = {10.1109/CSTE69562.2026.11649708},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048938181?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper presents a microservice-and-agentbased architecture for real-time personalization in distributed elearning systems. The study aims to address the limitations of conventional LMS/MOOC platforms, which predominantly rely on offline analytics and lack coordinated, low-latency adaptation under dynamic learner behavior and fluctuating system load. The objectives of this work are: (i) to design and validate an end-to-end event-driven pipeline for continuous collection and processing of learner behavioral data, (ii) to operationalize a coordinated agent layer that supports adaptive decision-making based on reinforcement learning policies, and (iii) to ensure predictable and conflict-free personalization through formal specification and verification of safety and liveness properties. The proposed architecture integrates microservice decomposition, an event bus for streaming learning events, and an agent layer implementing Q-learning and Proximal Policy Optimization for adaptive control. Learner behavior is modeled using a compact state-action-reward formulation derived from behavioral indicators, with optional enrichment via computer vision-based engagement signals. Formal correctness is achieved by expressing coordination constraints in temporal logics and validating agent interactions through model checking. The resulting framework provides a reproducible and extensible foundation for scalable, low-latency, and formally verified personalization in modern e-learning platforms.  © 2026 IEEE.},
