	title = {Architectural Trade-off Analysis of Modern Service Mesh Designs: A Qualitative ATAM Study},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {2025 2nd International Conference on Computational Intelligence, Communication Technology and Networking, CICTN 2025},
	pages = {873 - 880},
	doi = {10.1109/CICTN64563.2025.10932541},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105002693214?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Modern cloud native applications are highly dependent on service mesh designs, which provide solutions for communication, security, and observability. Architectural decisions for service mesh implementations involve numerous trade-offs that affect system quality. This study analyzes three popular service mesh architectures: Istio, Linkerd, and Consul Connect, using the Architecture Tradeoff Analysis Method (ATAM), we created a utility tree that considers essential quality attributes such as security, maintenance, reliability, and operability. Our analysis identifies important areas of sensitivity where architectural changes have a significant impact on quality attributes, as well as trade-off points where increasing one attribute affects others. Our research identifies common trends in how service mesh designs handle opposing requirements, including proxy deployment, control plane distribution, and configuration management approaches. Our analysis shows that, while all systems prioritize security and observability, they differ dramatically in how they handle the complexity-flexibility trade-off. This analysis offers a systematic approach for software architects to evaluate service mesh designs and understand their trade-offs, leading to better architectural decisions in microservices environments. © 2025 IEEE.},
