	abstract = {The main migration issues from monolithic architectures to microservices include various security challenges in the course of identifying service boundaries. Traditional process mining approaches focus on the identification of business process boundaries, leaving security-relevant execution patterns aside. This paper proposes a security-aware process mining approach complementing the Alpha++ algorithm by security pattern recognition, sensitive data flow tracking, and critical path extraction capabilities. Our Security-Aware Alpha++ (SA-Alpha++) algorithm generates security-annotated Petri nets explicitly modeling security-critical transitions and enables the identification of execution sequences requiring protected boundaries. Experimental evaluation on two industrial case studies comprising 156,000 lines of code shows that SA-Alpha++ achieves 94% security pattern coverage and 89% critical path identification accuracy, which represents improvements by 32-44% over traditional process mining algorithms. The critical security paths extracted are actionable and guide boundary placement decisions with respect to preserving security properties during decomposition. In a nutshell, our approach enables architects to visualize runtime security dependencies and make boundary decisions based on the actual execution behavior. © 2026 IEEE.},
